{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Your tithes</b> — the tithe of cattle and the second tithe, to be eaten within the precinct. <b>And the offering of your hand</b> — these are the first fruits, as it is said concerning them (Deuteronomy 26:4), \"And the priest shall take the basket from your hand\" — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 12:6), from the words of our Sages (Sifrei 12). And according to the plain sense, Scripture mentions the burnt offerings and the slaughtered offerings that one is obligated to eat there in the precinct before YHWH, and it also mentions that one shall bring there the tithe and the terumah, that one shall give them to the priests and the Levites, the ministers of the Sanctuary, and they too shall rejoice with you and with your households, as it is said (Malachi 3:10), \"Bring all the tithe into the storehouse, that there may be food in My house, and test Me now by this, said HaShem of Hosts, if I will not open for you the windows of heaven and pour out for you a blessing beyond measure.\" So too they established in the Second Temple concerning terumah and tithe of the tithe that they would bring them to the Sanctuary, as it is said (Nehemiah 10:38), \"And the first of our dough, and our terumot, and the fruit of every tree, the wine and the oil, we shall bring to the priests, to the chambers of the house of our God,\" and it is said (ibid. v. 39), \"to the house of our God, to the chambers, to the storehouse.\" And the reason for <b>the offering of your hand</b> is that the Torah did not specify a fixed amount for terumah, but rather it is according to whatever a person's hand raises. Similarly, what it says (Deuteronomy 12:17), \"You may not eat within your gates\" — it warns the Israelite not to eat within his gates the tithe, the firstborn, the votive offerings, and the terumah, and then it again distinguished among them (Deuteronomy 12:18), \"But you shall eat them before YHWH your Elohim, you and your son and your daughter\" — what may be eaten by you; \"and the Levite who is within your gates\" shall eat there, within your gates, what is fitting for him, as it is said concerning them (Numbers 18:31), \"And you shall eat it in every place,\" and Scripture is brief regarding matters that are explained in another place. But I am of the opinion, according to the plain sense of this verse, that <b>the offering of your hand</b> is what a person lifts up to HaShem from his silver and his gold, to make of it a burnt offering or a slaughtered offering. And Scripture mentioned three categories of offerings: the votive offerings — which are \"I vow a sacrifice of an ox, a sheep, or a goat\"; and the freewill offerings — where one says of an ox or a sheep, \"Behold, this is an offering,\" and brings it to the Sanctuary; and the terumah — which one lifts up from all that one has, silver or gold, or one brings one's unconsecrated animal to the courtyard and says to the priests, \"Behold, this is from my hand to HaShem.\" It is in the language of \"whoever raises a terumah of silver and bronze, they brought the terumah of YHWH\" (Exodus 35:24), and likewise, \"All the terumot of the holy things that the children of Israel shall lift up to HaShem\" (Numbers 18:19) — they are offerings. So too it says concerning the shekels (Exodus 30:13), \"Half a shekel after the shekel of the Sanctuary… a terumah to HaShW,\" and they are for the offerings (Megillah 29b). For whatever a person lifts up to HaShem from all that he has — whether for offerings, or for the maintenance of the House, as in \"They shall take for Me a terumah\" (Exodus 25:2), or for the priests, the ministers of HaShem — all of it is called terumah, as in what is written concerning the booty (Numbers 31:29), \"You shall give to Eleazar the priest the terumah of HaShem,\" and they were entirely unconsecrated in his hand. And likewise, \"I weighed out to them the silver and the gold and the vessels, the terumah of the house of our Elohim, which the king and his counselors had raised\" (Ezra 8:25). And Scripture did not say <b>your terumot</b>, so that one not err by thinking of the priests' terumah, but rather it said <b>the offering of your hand</b> — what you donate willingly from what comes into your hand. And behold, all the items mentioned here are obligated in the precinct, for the meaning of \"your tithes\" is the tithe of cattle and the second tithe, which are eaten before HaShem, for the first tithe is the tithe of the Levites, and the tithe of the poor belongs to them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:48:57.455928300Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}